# Jev classification

Closed decisions go to TypeSafe System One (Jev). Open text stays on the general model. One module in the repo is allowed to call `https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone`.

## Which job goes where

Send Jev a fixed menu: `choice` (one label), `noul` (yes/no), `score` (a point on a scale). One POST per input when the questions share that input. Add a question to the existing call. A second request is only for a different input.

Keep the general model for work that must produce new text: transcription, dates and titles, amounts, invoice lines, rewritten prose.

Auth is a TypeSafe console key (`apikey_…`) in `JEV_API_KEY`. Do not use a hosted `jv_live_` key against this endpoint.

## One door, then the SDK

The first classification may be a small hand-built POST.

```python
body = json.dumps({
    "model": "jev-latest",
    "state": state,
    "questions": questions,
}).encode("utf-8")
request = Request(
    "https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone",
    data=body,
    method="POST",
    headers={
        "Authorization": f"Bearer {api_key}",
        "Content-Type": "application/json",
    },
)
```

When a second question set is added, switch that one function to `typesafe-sdk` before a third copy appears. Every feature calls that shared door.

- Questions are `Choice`, `Noul`, and `Score` objects, so a new set cannot drift into a hand-built dict with the wrong shape.
- The questions stay next to the feature that owns them. The door only sends them.
- The SDK retries rate limits and server errors. That matters once classification sits on more than one path.

```python
# feature module owns the questions
intent = Choice(instructions="...", criteria={"freeform": "...", "todo": "..."})

# the single door sends them
decide(state, questions)
```
